     {q:"Marginal revenue can be found by taking the ___ of the total revenue function.", options:["A calculation entirely unrelated to derivatives","Sum","Derivative","Product"], answer:2},
     {q:"Why might a business use marginal cost and marginal revenue to help determine an optimal level of production?", options:["Comparing these rates of change can help identify the production level where profit is maximized","Marginal cost and marginal revenue provide no useful information for business decisions","This concept has no connection to real-world economic decision-making","Businesses never use any mathematical analysis when determining production levels"], answer:0},
     {q:"Why is calculus a useful mathematical tool for analyzing economic concepts like marginal cost and revenue?", options:["Calculus provides tools for precisely analyzing rates of change, which are central to how these economic concepts are defined","This concept only applies to purely theoretical mathematics with no economic application","Marginal cost and marginal revenue can never be analyzed using calculus","Calculus has no meaningful application to the field of economics"], answer:0},
     {q:"Why might a company aim to produce at the point where marginal cost equals marginal revenue?", options:["This condition has no connection to a company’s profit","This concept has no relevance to economic decision-making","Companies should always aim to produce as much as physically possible with no other consideration","This is often the production level associated with maximizing overall profit"], answer:3}
